{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## CLOSING — 14/14 criteria met, exit bar verified on `main`, 59/59 descendants closed and certified\n\n### The load-bearing evidence\n\n**Harness run 30986591446 at `0df44dcbc9` (on `main`): 62 passed, 1 xfailed, 0 failed, 0 ERRORED**,\ncanary `collected=63 executed=62 skipped=1` — so nothing was silently skipped behind a green tick.\nVerified from the job log, not from the run's conclusion.\n\n**59 descendants, all closed, ZERO uncertified** — every one carrying a genuine\n`completion-verifier` signature. No `--force-close` was used anywhere in this cycle. That was the\nwhole point: this epic reopened [rebar:b586-2fda-8a15-4260], [rebar:0059-c7f0-cce1-4077] and\n[rebar:2bc7-...] precisely because they had been closed without one.\n\n### The exit bar earned its place twice\n\nIt was added because 13 criteria were ALL met while three live DC cells failed — none required the\nharness to pass, so criterion-completeness was not evidence the feature worked. Those three\n(`inbound_set_subtask_parent`, `outbound_set_subtask_parent`, `outbound_clear_parent`) had failed\n**12 of 12** harness runs and were resolved by [rebar:9f26-66c2-14af-4223] and\n[rebar:4b9e-bcad-456a-4233] — re-homed to the mechanism DC actually supports, with NO cell deleted,\nskipped or `xfail`ed.\n\nThen it caught a regression introduced AFTER it was written: [rebar:9790-cafa-dffa-462e]'s readiness\nrework errored all 62 cells at setup, and 9790 had met all five of its own criteria. A criteria set\nthat does not include *the thing still works* can be fully satisfied by a change that breaks it.\nFixed under [rebar:941b-f049-5f29-4410].\n\n### What the delivered feature actually does now\n\nDC parent/hierarchy sync reaches Jira **through a real reconcile pass**\n(`test_outbound_epic_parent_reaches_dc_THROUGH_A_RECONCILE_PASS`), converges rather than churning\n(`..._plans_nothing` on a repeat pass), and **refuses loudly** where the platform cannot comply\ninstead of reporting a false success — which was the real user-visible defect: DC accepts a sub-task\n`fields.parent` write with HTTP 204 and silently ignores it, so a user's hierarchy change was lost\nwith no signal.\n\n### Measurement beat documentation FOUR times\n\n* the capability map's 255-char label ceiling — retracted (1389)\n* req-0022's duplicate-`Task` ids — retracted (1394), and a **SAME-DIGEST** contradiction, the one\n  class a digest gate provably cannot catch\n* Cloud's label ceiling — live probe showed 255 accepted / 256 rejected, IDENTICAL to DC, so\n  [rebar:2c93-ce1c-127e-4d58]'s proposed per-deployment ceiling was machinery for a difference that\n  never existed\n* 9790's own error message asserted a decision rule (\"no `customfield_*` ⇒ plugin still starting ⇒\n  budget too short\") that was wrong AND self-confirming, since an empty custom-field inventory is\n  exactly what a projectless instance returns. It misdiagnosed its author, then me, then the ticket I\n  wrote from it. The real cause was an ordering deadlock and the fix LOWERED the budget 600s -> 120s.\n\n### Follow-on work, homed rather than orphaned\n\n[rebar:5796-d1f8-870f-4dc1] (`depends_on` this epic) holds [rebar:4a6d-5bbc-44f4-4a56],\n[rebar:a3fa-...] and [rebar:f020-0960-2c42-4d61]. Out of scope by deliberate decision: the\nCloud-surface parity defects (6194, 6a74, 9a46), the general vacuous-guard class\n([rebar:8a5e-b88e-0c3e-4544], third occurrence), and [rebar:a9bd-6641-e603-42bc]."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1785922233206070001,"uuid":"cd2cfec3-7bdb-4983-9d90-b2200a36a64a"}